STATUS: PARSER WORK PENDING / FEATURE UNCERTAIN

This test exercises **compiler annotation syntax on type positions** —
specifically `source: Source[text]` on event declarations, and (originally)
the `[type]"path"` source-block annotation on the call site.

## Architectural context

Compiler annotations are a DIFFERENT system from phantom types:

- **Phantom types** (now `<>`) — value-level, runtime-flowing, semantic-check-
  triggering. Labels flow with values, trigger discharge events, propagate
  through arithmetic. E.g. `*File<opened!>`, `f32<celsius>`.
- **Compiler annotations** (kept on `[]`) — declaration-level metadata for
  the compiler/transforms. NOT runtime-flowing. NOT phantom-checker-relevant.
  E.g. `[!]` event-default marker, `[T:u32;N:1024]` generic params,
  `[comptime|transform]` event modifiers, `Source[text]` source-shape hint.

See `docs/COMPILER_ANNOTATIONS.md` for the catalog of current annotation uses.

## Why this test is currently failing

The phantom syntax cutover (`[label]` → `<label>` for phantom types,
2026-05-29) removed the only parser path that was extracting `[...]` content
from type strings — that path was doing double duty for both phantoms and
compiler annotations, conflating the two in storage. Removing it for the
phantom cutover ALSO removed the only mechanism stripping annotations from
type strings before Zig emission.

Result: `Source[text]` now flows through to Zig as `Source[text]`, which
Zig reads as array indexing into the type `Source` and rejects with
"use of undeclared identifier 'text'".

## Why we're not removing the test yet

Removing it would leave residue in the parser (the source-block syntax
extraction at `src/parser.zig:2842-2861` is still there) without a test
exercising it, AND commits us to dropping a feature we might want later.

Compiler annotations on type positions are used rarely today (10+ stdlib
events use bare `Source`, only this one experiments with `Source[text]`),
but the annotation system as a whole is real and useful — the language
just hasn't decided whether to expand or prune that surface.

## The open decision

Two real paths forward, neither urgent:

1. **Build annotation-extraction properly** — add `[]` extraction to the
   parser, routing to a new AST field (`annotation` separate from `phantom`).
   Downstream consumers (transform passes that want shape hints) read from
   the new field. ~30-100 LOC parser work. Test goes green.

2. **Drop the compiler-annotation-on-type-position feature entirely** —
   remove `src/parser.zig:2842-2861` (source-block syntax), remove this
   test, accept that bare `Source` is the only supported shape and that
   `Source[text]` was an experiment that didn't earn its keep.

Lars's current read (2026-05-29): on the fence. The annotation system has
many legitimate uses (`[!]`, generics, comptime modifiers), but
annotations on TYPE positions specifically are rare. Wants to defer the
call rather than commit either way.
